t(2, 1420, """In the words of our Great Lord, holy are His names and attributes, exalted in the heavens and the earth, high above all, greatly exalted:
"And We did not send into any town a warner except that its affluent ones said: Indeed we, in what you were sent with, are disbelievers."
The way of our Great Lord with those who turn away from the signs was fulfilled in the affluent, and those affluent had minds that thought in the following way:

First:
"Shall we believe in you while you are followed by the lowest?"
Fear for what they regard as their social class, so they fear being made equal with the poor.

Second:
"And they said: We are more in wealth and children, and we are not to be punished."
Considering success in gaining wealth in this world a sign of Allah's pleasure; so in their view they will enjoy bliss in this world and the Hereafter, and nothing awaits them in the Hereafter.

Third:
Affluence breeds a life of ease and moral laxity; so they rejected any way that restricts their desires or imposes on them "lawful and unlawful" and strips them of the freedom of frivolity they were used to, because the ways of the Prophets are a divine law with its own rules.

For these reasons the affluent were always the last to believe, and when they believed, they believed together with the people, not individually.

As for the men of wealth who believed in the Prophet ﷺ, they were not affluent but men of wealth; the affluent one is one thing and the man of wealth is another.

The affluent one is the person whom wealth has seduced.""")

