t(4, 60, """surplus wealth. My word is never for these; these words are only for those of whom one can go on a single leisure trip and spend on it the lifetime "savings" of a person who is well-off and not poor.
-------------------------

There are verses and definitive Sharia proofs that make charity (beyond the obligatory zakat) obligatory, a personal obligation, on the rich in times of war, disaster and famine.
The jurists of Islam agree on a famous principle derived from the Qur'an and Sunnah which states: "There is a due on wealth other than zakat"; meaning that if the annual zakat (2.5%) does not cover the need of the Muslims in calamities and wars, the rich must give more of their wealth until want and harm are removed.

Today in Palestine, Yemen, Sudan, the camps of Syria and the camps of the Rohingya there are people eating sand, literally and not figuratively.

These are calamities. These are not cases of poverty. In cases of poverty the rich man has a choice, but in cases of calamity the rich man has no choice.

The most prominent verses and proofs the scholars relied on to make charity obligatory in times of calamity and war:
## 1. Proof from Qur'anic verses

* Emphatic obligation in times of famine and war (masghaba):
Allah the Exalted said: "But he has not attempted the steep path. And what will make you know what the steep path is? It is freeing a slave, or feeding on a day of severe hunger an orphan of near kin, or a needy person in misery" [Surat al-Balad: 11-16].
"The day of masghaba" is the day of severe famine and distress arising from wars and siege, and Allah made feeding the hungry here the only way to cross the "steep path" and be saved from the Fire.

* The prohibition of the miserliness that leads to ruin:
Allah the Exalted said: "And spend in the way of Allah and do not throw yourselves with your own hands into ruin" [Surat al-Baqarah: 195]. This verse was revealed urging the rich to spend on equipping armies and helping those harmed by wars, and the Qur'an considered refraining from spending in these cases throwing oneself into ruin and destruction.

## 2. Sayings of the verifying scholars
* Imam Ibn Taymiyyah:
He stressed in [Majmu' al-Fatawa] that zakat is due because of the wealth itself, but there are other obligations due on wealth for incidental reasons, saying: "Giving is obligatory in a calamity, and feeding the hungry and clothing the naked are obligatory as a communal duty."

* Imam al-Qurtubi the Maliki:
He stated explicitly in his tafsir: "The scholars agree that if a need befalls the Muslims after zakat has been paid, the wealth must be spent on it." And he used it as proof that ransoming captives and relieving the distressed is obligatory even if it consumes all the wealth of the rich.

* Imam al-Ghazali:
He explained in al-Mustasfa that if the state treasury (Bayt al-Mal) is empty and an enemy or famine befalls the Muslims, it is permissible to impose compulsory financial levies on the rich beyond the amount of zakat to secure the sufficiency and protection of society.

## Summary
Charity in time of peace and plenty is voluntary and recommended, but in times of war, siege, famine and general calamity it leaves the level of recommendation for the level of a personal and binding obligation, definitively due on every rich person who has surplus wealth; because protecting the lives of Muslims and warding off ruin from them comes before protecting the ownership of wealth.

There are severe punishments and stern threats in the Noble Qur'an and the Prophetic Sunnah for people of wealth who refrain from spending and feeding the needy in cases of famine, general calamity and the needs of society.
These punishments range between the threat of punishment in the Hereafter and worldly punishments (whether legislated or decreed), as follows:
## First: Punishments and threats in the Noble Qur'an
* Entering Hell directly because of not feeding: the Noble Qur'an made refraining from feeding the hungry in a time of need a main reason for entering Saqar. Allah the Exalted said in the dialogue of the people of Paradise with the criminals: "What put you into Saqar? They will say: We were not of those who prayed, nor did we feed the poor" [Surat al-Muddaththir: 42-44].
* Deprivation of intercession on the Day of Resurrection: Allah the Exalted said about one who owns wealth and does not help the needy with it: "Indeed, he did not believe in Allah, the Most Great, nor did he urge the feeding of the poor. So no devoted friend has he here this Day, nor any food except from the discharge of wounds" [Surat al-Haqqah: 33-36].
* A decreed worldly punishment (ruin of wealth): Allah the Exalted gave in [Surat al-Qalam] the story of the "owners of the garden" (the owners of the orchard) who agreed among themselves not to let a single poor person enter their orchard or eat of its fruit on harvest day. The immediate worldly punishment was that Allah sent upon it a fire that burned it completely while they slept: "So there came upon it an affliction from your Lord while they were asleep, and it became as if reaped" [Surat al-Qalam: 19-20].""")

t(5, 105, """## Second: Punishments and threats in the Prophetic Sunnah
* The lifting of Allah's guarantee and protection from the community: the Prophet ﷺ held that a community in which one hungry person spends the night while rich people are present has Allah's protection and care lifted from it. He ﷺ said: "Any people of a courtyard (a neighbourhood or community) among whom a person wakes up hungry, the protection of Allah the Exalted is withdrawn from them" (narrated by Ahmad).
* Negation of complete faith: the Prophet ﷺ negated complete faith from one who knows of his neighbour's need and hunger and spends the night full. He ﷺ said: "He has not believed in me who spends the night full while his neighbour beside him is hungry and he knows it" (narrated by al-Tabarani and al-Bazzar).

* Punishment by withholding rain and general famine: the Prophetic Sunnah linked the miserliness of the rich with economic crises and famines, as in the hadith of Ibn Umar: "...and they do not cheat in measure and weight except that they are seized with years (of famine), hardship of provision and the tyranny of the ruler over them" (narrated by Ibn Majah).

## Third: Legislative and legal punishment (the authority of those in charge)
From the applied fiqh perspective, if the rich refuse to relieve the hunger of the starving and help society in calamities and wars, the Sharia has punishments and compulsory measures taken by the judge or the ruler of the Muslims:

1. Taking by force: the jurists agree that the ruler has the right to take the surplus wealth of the rich by compulsion and to take it from them to feed the hungry and equip the defence if the need of society becomes specific, and this is not considered injustice but the very essence of justice to protect lives.

2. Discretionary and bodily punishment or imprisonment: the person who refuses to pay the maintenance and charity due on him toward society is punished with imprisonment or a fine until he submits to the right of the poor and the distressed.

3. Applying the rulings on crimes (causing death): some jurists (such as Ibn Hazm and the Malikis) held that if a rich man knows of a person on the brink of perishing from hunger or cold and does not save him though able to, and the poor man dies, the rich man is liable for the blood money of the dead, because he withheld from him a due right he was able to give and so became a cause of his death.

- How did the Rightly Guided Caliphs (such as Umar ibn al-Khattab in the Year of Ashes) apply these punishments and exceptional measures to manage the famine?

The Rightly Guided Caliphs, foremost among them Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, during "the Year of Ashes" (18 AH), gave the finest examples of firm management of crises and famines. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, did not settle for preaching; he used the legislative and executive authority of the state to apply strict punishments and measures aimed at protecting society, compelling those who fell short to spend and relieving the hunger of the starving.

## Obliging the rich to forced equalisation and social integration
When more than 60 thousand refugees poured in from the desert and camped around Madinah fleeing hunger, Umar exhausted all the funds of the Bayt al-Mal (the public treasury). Then he set a plan to compel all the households of the rich and well-off in Madinah to spend directly.

Islamic history and legislation include established cases in which wealth was taken from the rich by force and by the power of law and Sharia, and the matter went beyond mere preaching and voluntary sharing; for the social right in Islamic fiqh is not begging, but a right taken by the authority of the state if the rich man refuses to give it in calamities.

## 1. The practical measure of [Umar ibn al-Khattab] (taking in time of need)
In the Year of Ashes, Umar did not leave the matter to the consciences of the rich; rather it is established from him that he took the surplus food of the rich, their camels and livestock, by the power of public order.
* He applied this in practice when he took over the surplus camels and livestock of the Bayt al-Mal from the reserved pastures and outlying areas and drove them to be slaughtered to feed the people in the squares of Madinah.

## 2. The ruling of Imam Ibn Hazm al-Andalusi (taking by force)
Imam Ibn Hazm (in his famous book [al-Muhalla]) is one of the most explicit jurists who laid down this matter historically and practically, as he stated that taking by force is obligatory if the rich man refuses, and his decisive ruling says:

"It is obligatory on the rich of every land to take care of their poor, and the ruler compels them to do so if the zakat and the rest of the Muslims' wealth do not suffice them; so they are given food they need to eat, clothing... and a dwelling that shelters them."

## 3. Judicial application: financial liability (the crime of withholding)
It is established in Islamic judiciary (among the Malikis and Hanbalis) what is known as holding the rich liable.
* The form of the ruling applied historically: if a hungry or desperate man passes by a rich man who has food beyond his need and asks him to sell or help, and the rich man refuses him until the poor man dies of hunger, the judge rules against the rich man with the blood money of manslaughter or intentional killing (as the case may be), and the blood money is taken by force from the rich man's estate or wealth.
This financial taking is a criminal penalty established by rulings, because withholding surplus wealth in calamities is considered the crime of killing by causation.""")

t(6, 95, """## 4. The story of the Army of Hardship (taking half by force)
The Prophet ﷺ commanded the rich to spend to equip the army at Tabuk (a time of hardship, famine and straits), and [Umar ibn al-Khattab] brought half his wealth and Abu Bakr brought all his wealth. But in other situations it is established that the Prophet ﷺ legislated financial punishment by taking part of the refuser's wealth; he ﷺ said about one who withholds obligatory financial dues: "We will take it and half of his wealth, a binding right among the rights of our Lord" (narrated by Abu Dawud and al-Nasa'i), which is proof that it is permissible to confiscate half of a rich man's wealth as discipline and punishment for his miserliness and withholding the rights of society and the state.

## Summary
The settled fiqh principle in calamities and disasters is: "The wealth of the rich is protected, unless it conflicts with protecting innocent lives, for protecting lives comes before safeguarding wealth." Therefore it is established in fiqh and in the judiciary that the ruler may take this wealth by force and compulsion, not by way of voluntary donation.""")

