t(9, 1420, """A year ago I wished for two things, and months ago I wished for a third. About two weeks ago I saw a vision of the Lord of Might, the Great, holy are His names and attributes, exalted in the heavens and the earth, high above all, greatly exalted, speaking. I saw myself in the vision saying: if only I could do such and such, and if only such and such would happen to me. What I meant in my vision to do was not among the extraordinary things, while what would happen to me was among the extraordinary things. Then a voice came to me which I knew was the voice of the Great Lord, holy are His names and attributes, saying: {{{"You have been granted your request ..."}}}, and both matters are of the future, not at this time nor at this stage of life.

As for the second, hard one, which was less than a week ago and which I published, it is the word the Great Lord said in it:
"I will punish him with a severe punishment"; then the image of a person appeared. I want to make clear that this is a vision whose details I am forbidden to go into; I mentioned it only as a severe admonition and lesson because of the gravity of the calamity, for the image of the man was of someone who prays the five prayers. So is there anyone who takes a lesson?! Is there anyone who takes heed? Is there anyone who fears as fear is due? Is there anyone who reflects and ponders and whose mind has woken? For most, not all, Muslims have sleeping minds! Will we wake up, or will we go on like this doing what we wish to Allah's creation as individuals within our societies and saying: we pray all the prayers in the first row?!

Is there anything more terrifying than this?! No, by Allah; although I have been seeing visions since 2003, the visions of the Great Lord speaking, holy are His names and attributes, exalted in the heavens and the earth, high above all, greatly exalted, only happened to me in the last two weeks; and by Allah, ordinary visions never, ever resemble the visions of the Great Lord speaking in sleep.

Visions in which the Great Lord does not speak in sleep, set in contexts we are used to hearing from people or used to seeing ourselves, are one world; and visions in which the Great Lord speaks are another, completely different world.
They are not an ordinary kind, neither at the level of feeling and the grip that seizes the soul and heart and wakes you up aware that the One who spoke to you is the Great Lord, nor at the level of value.

Visions of the Lord speaking are decisive and definite and admit no interpretation; they are not symbols to decode that bear a thousand meanings, but a direct, clear word that bears only one meaning.

Prayer is one of the conditions for entering Paradise by the mercy of Allah the Exalted, but what about those who do injustice and transgress?!

O people .. this is a statement and this is an admonition, and Allah the Exalted alone is behind the intent.""")

h(10, 500, """My experience with visions of being spoken to
The first vision
The second vision""")

t(10, 1110, """Being spoken to in a dream vision is possible and established in Sharia; indeed, being spoken to by a voice, or the casting of words into the soul (heart) of the servant during sleep, is the most common and clearest form the scholars mentioned in explaining seeing Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in a dream.

To understand the matter of being spoken to in a dream precisely, the scholars clarified the following:

1. Allah speaking to His servants in a dream is a kind of glad tidings

Hearing the speech of Allah the Exalted in a dream, as guidance, glad tidings or a warning, is considered of the kind of "glad tidings" that remain of prophethood, as the Prophet ﷺ said: "Nothing remains of prophethood except glad tidings." They said: And what are the glad tidings? He said: "The righteous dream" (narrated by al-Bukhari).

2. The ruling on the hearing and how it is classified

Its occurring in the heart: it happens in the soul of the dreamer, with full certainty while asleep, that the speaker is Allah, Majestic and Exalted, so he hears words commanding him to good, giving him glad tidings of Paradise, or forgiving him a sin, without seeing a being or a form.

It is not like Allah's speech to His messengers while awake: Allah speaking to the servant in a dream is not like the speaking specific to the Prophets while awake (such as Allah's speech to Musa, peace be upon him, or to our Prophet Muhammad ﷺ in the Isra and Mi'raj); the speech in a dream is a "dream vision" that falls under the heading of striking parables in hearts, not a legislative address and general revelation to the Ummah.

If the speech is a command to obey, a prohibition of a sin, a making firm on the truth, or glad tidings of forgiveness, then it is truth and a true vision.

A famous story from the Salaf

It is narrated that Imam Ahmad ibn Hanbal, may Allah have mercy on him, said: "I saw the Lord of Might in a dream and said: O Lord, what is the best thing by which those who draw near draw near to You? The Great said: By My speech, O Ahmad (that is, the Qur'an). I said: O Lord, with understanding or without understanding? The Great said: With understanding and without understanding."

The scholars cite this story to show that being spoken to in a dream happens to the righteous by way of making them firm and giving glad tidings, or warning and guidance.""")

h(11, 300, """The Lord of Might speaking to His servants in sleep
is established""")

t(11, 875, """It is established in Islamic Sharia that a Muslim may enter the Fire despite keeping the five prayers, if he commits major sins and wrongs connected with injustice to people or their rights, or does things that nullify the reward of prayer, and Allah does not pardon him.

The five prayers are a great cause for entering Paradise and being saved from the Fire, but they are not an absolute cheque for committing forbidden things without account; the disobedient Muslim is under the will of Allah if he does not repent.

Proofs from the Prophetic Sunnah
The groups who may pray and enter the Fire:

1. The bankrupt on the Day of Resurrection (injustice to people)

The most prominent proof is the hadith of the Prophet ﷺ about "the bankrupt", where he said: "The bankrupt of my Ummah is one who comes on the Day of Resurrection with prayer, fasting and zakat, but comes having insulted this one, slandered that one, consumed the wealth of this one, shed the blood of that one and beaten this one; so this one is given of his good deeds and that one of his good deeds, and if his good deeds run out before what is against him is settled, some of their sins are taken and thrown on him, then he is thrown into the Fire" (narrated by Muslim). Here we find the person prays but enters the Fire because of people's rights.

2. Harming neighbours

The Prophet ﷺ was asked about a woman who prays, gives charity and fasts much, but harms her neighbours with her tongue, and he ﷺ said: "She is in the Fire" (narrated by Ahmad and al-Bazzar). This woman prayed the five prayers and added supererogatory ones, yet was not saved from the Fire because of her bad character and her harming.""")

h(11, 2100, """Even if he prays the five in the first row!
Those who have lost themselves!""")

t(12, 225, """How do we understand history?
A stone in still water, by the power of Allah the Great, by His repelling some people by others, then many decreed variables, then results that were the last thing the minds of realists could expect!

The world was struck by a "little ice age" that extended through the lifetime of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ (570 - 632 CE), known scientifically and historically as the Late Antique Little Ice Age.

Geological and historical climate studies published in respected scientific journals have shown that this cold period began around 536 CE and continued until 660 CE.
To understand the nature of this little ice age and its effect on the world and the Arabian Peninsula in the time of prophethood, here are the most important details:
## 1. How did this little ice age begin?
* Successive volcanic eruptions: it began with the effect of a severe volcanic winter in 536 CE (about 34 years before the birth of the Prophet ﷺ), followed by other eruptions in 540 and 547 CE.
* Blocking of the sun's rays: these volcanoes pumped huge quantities of sulphur gas and dust into the atmosphere, which blocked sunlight for long periods (up to 18 months in some regions), so temperatures fell sharply in the northern hemisphere.

## 2. Its effect on the Arabian Peninsula and the rise of Islam
According to historical and climate research, the effect of this climate change on the Arabian Peninsula was different and decisive:

* Increased rainfall: unlike Europe, which suffered frost and famines, global cooling in that period changed the paths of rain-bearing winds, which increased humidity and brought heavier rain to the northern and central parts of the Arabian Peninsula (the Hijaz region and its surroundings).
* Revival of vegetation and pastures: these rains helped fertile pastures grow and provided water resources, which contributed to a demographic and economic leap and made travel across the desert easier. Some historians and climate scientists hold that these favourable environmental conditions were a supporting geographic factor in backing the movement of the early Islamic conquests and uniting the Arab tribes under one banner.""")

