t(15, 60, """To those who do not excuse people for what they see of others' states that they dislike, though it is not their right in the first place to excuse or not excuse, and to those who think ill of people; from what I met years ago in life:

How many a story you are heedless of?! And how many a tale is hidden from you?! So do not rely too much on your minds to know the truths, otherwise no one would tire if the answer were always in the mind!

The image of this post has a story and a tale that lasted 34 years and began at the end of the age of 17!

The 17th juz shown in the image reflects a recitation between Surat al-Anbiya and Surat al-Hajj, in an unintended reading in the mosque during the time between the adhan and the iqamah for the Isha prayer today.

34 years, praise be to Allah, of prayer and commitment to it at home, but with a bitter experience in being able to keep going to the mosque to perform the prayers other than Friday. The reason was exhaustion from a particular magic meant to make reaching the mosque difficult and to leave the body very worn out and struck with a temporary inability to set out to the mosque at prayer time; then the tiredness would vanish when the prayer in the mosque ended!

This story did not have one, two or three lessons only. It is a life journey. The tale of a hard path that tells us well that our brothers and sisters afflicted by magic do not suffer something simple or easy, ever.

It tells us how many people easily drag themselves into thinking ill and hurling accusations. It teaches us how a person makes a fool of himself when he thinks that what his eye sees is the absolute truth just because his eye saw it!

It is the tale of a great longing and a painful path of a young man who grew up and was raised in mosques and was then prevented from reaching them regularly for 34 years .. regularly, not completely.

Today time took me back, literally and not figuratively, to the year 1992. Not figuratively. As if I were in the mosque of Sheikh Ibrahim, may Allah have mercy on him, who taught me tajwid. It is the story of someone deprived for 34 years of a beautiful thing .. regularity .. regularity in the mosque .. regularity in the mosque is not like merely being passing by on the road and the prayer time comes, so you get out of your car to pray. That does not resemble the beauty of regularity, nor the sweetness of regularity, nor the delight of regularity.

O you who are not prevented and whom nothing holds back but laziness or not being convinced of the beauty of the mosque and prayer in it: read this tale of mine! Read it to know that what you can do easily, but are indifferent to or lazy about, there are people whose number only Allah the Exalted knows who lived long years wishing for this thing; and when Allah enabled them, like this poor servant, by the grace of Allah the Exalted, I went to the mosque yesterday in the first days of regularity, repeating on the way: Labbayk Allahumma labbayk .. It is a joy unlike any other joy I have passed through since 1992, not even any joy of whatever kind I have lived since that year.

Juz 17 after 34 years have passed since the break in regularity at age 17 .. a message that was an answer to a question I asked myself today between the Maghrib and Isha prayers while inside the mosque: why do I feel as if I am in the year 1992?

O Muslim men and Muslim women:
Do not forget in your prostrations your brothers and sisters afflicted by magic, for whoever has not witnessed it will not understand, and whoever understood understood because he witnessed and saw with his own eyes and in himself; it is hard to make understand someone who does not want to understand, and whoever is arrogant and does not want to understand will, if Allah wills, never understand in his life. So your brothers need your prayers.""")

t(16, 345, """Labbayk Allahumma labbayk .. O Allah, relieve the distress of the distressed and lift the magic, O our Lord, O our God, O our Master, from those afflicted by magic""")

t(16, 1475, """The houses of Allah the Great are tranquillity and successive mercies, a spot of the earth the souls long for, in which the selves find comfort, to which hearts withdraw and with which chests are made happy. So, O Allah, lift from all those afflicted by magic whatever magic has been set upon them. O Allah, lift from them the magic of harm that strikes them and keeps them from the mosques, for they, O Most Merciful, are in great distress; they have longed for Your houses, and their souls have wept and moaned for years and years, while they wish to return to them, but the tyrants of the earth have exhausted them with what they did to them.""")

